	"nodes":[
		{"id":"9d4babeb5a3c8f10","x":-440,"y":-280,"width":900,"height":540,"type":"text","text":"# Independent Module Development Workflow\n\n> **For:** Any developer adding a new module to this project.\n> **Stack:** FastAPI + SQLAlchemy async + PostgreSQL + React + Zustand\n> **Architecture:** Modular monolith with onion (DDD) layering\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architecture at a Glance\n\nWe share one PostgreSQL database, but each module owns its own code. Table ownership is strict — one module writes, others read.\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    PostgreSQL (shared)                   │\n│  ┌──────────┐  ┌────────────────────┐  ┌─────────────┐  │\n│  │  users   │  │ ideas  idea_stages │  │research_jobs│  │\n│  └──────────┘  └────────────────────┘  └─────────────┘  │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n       ▲                  ▲                     ▲\n       │                  │                     │\n  ┌────┴───┐      ┌───────┴──────┐      ┌───────┴──────┐\n  │  auth  │      │    ideas     │      │   research   │\n  │ module │      │   module     │      │   module     │\n  └────────┘      └──────────────┘      └──────────────┘\n       │                  ▲\n       │ Depends()        │ reads (own ORM)\n       ▼                  │\n  ┌────────────────────────────────┐\n  │  onboarding / business-plan /  │\n  │  mentors / any future module   │\n  └────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n**Key rules:**\n- `auth` → owns `users` table, injected into all other modules via `Depends(get_current_user)`\n- `ideas` → owns `ideas` + `idea_stages` tables, is the **stage ledger** for all 12 stages\n- Every other module reads from `ideas` table using **its own ORM model** — never imports `ideas` module code\n- `shared/` → only `config.py`, `database.py`, `exceptions.py` — no business logic\n\n---\n\n## 2. Data Contract Standard\n\nEvery alembic migration file **must** include a `DATA CONTRACT` docstring. This is the only documentation a dependent module developer needs to start building — no code reading required.\n\n**Template:**\n\n```python\n\"\"\"<short description of what this migration does>\n\nDATA CONTRACT — <table_name> table\n- <column>: <type, range if numeric, lifecycle — when is it set? when is it null?>\n- <column>: <type, range if numeric, lifecycle>\n...\n\"\"\"\n```\n\n**Real example from this project** (`0005_add_ideas_table.py`):\n\n```python\n\"\"\"add ideas table and migrate from founder_profiles\n\nDATA CONTRACT — ideas table\n- onboarding_completed: bool, false by default. Set to true only after\n  POST /api/onboarding/complete succeeds (all 7 required fields present).\n- review_confidence: integer 0–100, null until POST /api/onboarding/review is called.\n  Non-null means the AI reviewer has run. 0 = very low confidence, 100 = very high.\n- review_summary: text, null until review runs. Non-null means review completed.\n- title: auto-set from idea_description on first step save. Can be null on a brand-new idea.\n- startup_stage: one of 'idea' | 'mvp' | 'growth' | null\n- user_id: FK to users.id — always set, never null\n- current_idea_id on users: the idea this user is actively working on. Null = no active idea.\n\"\"\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Starting a New Module — Step by Step\n\nWe'll use the **business-plan module** (Stage 3) as a worked example throughout.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1 — Identify dependent modules, read their DATA CONTRACTs\n\nThe business-plan module needs idea data (description, domain, review_summary) to generate a plan.\n\n1. Open `backend/alembic/versions/` — find the migration for the table you need\n2. Read the `DATA CONTRACT` docstring — this tells you what fields are safe to use and when\n3. You do **not** need to read any Python module code\n\n```\n# business-plan needs:\nbackend/alembic/versions/0005_add_ideas_table.py  ← read DATA CONTRACT for ideas table\nbackend/alembic/versions/0006_add_idea_stages_table.py  ← read DATA CONTRACT for idea_stages\n```\n\n**What you now know without reading any module code:**\n- `review_summary` is null until review ran → only start plan generation if it's non-null\n- `onboarding_completed` must be true before research/plan stages make sense\n- `current_idea_id` on the user tells you which idea to use\n\n---\n\n### Step 2 — Brainstorm your schema and API contract\n\nTell Claude Code (or discuss with the team):\n\n> \"I'm building a `business_plan` module for Stage 3. It reads `idea_description`, `domain`, and `review_summary` from the `ideas` table, then generates 6 plan sections (idea, product, phases, financials, prototype, mvp). I need a migration, DATA CONTRACT, and the module folder structure.\"\n\nClaude will design your schema, ORM model, ports, use cases, and router with your project's conventions.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3 — Create your module folder\n\n```\nbackend/app/modules/business_plan/\n├── domain/\n│   ├── entities.py         # BusinessPlan, BPSection dataclasses — pure Python, no DB imports\n│   └── ports.py            # IBusinessPlanRepo, IIdeaReader — Protocol interfaces only\n├── application/\n│   └── use_cases.py        # GeneratePlanUseCase, GetPlanUseCase — depends only on domain\n├── infrastructure/\n│   ├── repository.py       # PostgresBusinessPlanRepo — implements IBusinessPlanRepo\n│   └── idea_reader.py      # PostgresIdeaReader — implements IIdeaReader, YOUR OWN ORM model\n└── presentation/\n    ├── router.py            # FastAPI routes, injects Depends(get_current_user)\n    └── schemas.py           # Pydantic request/response models\n```\n\n**Layer dependency rule:**\n\n```\ndomain ← application ← infrastructure\ndomain ← application ← presentation\n```\n\n- `domain` imports nothing from this project\n- `application` imports only from `domain`\n- `infrastructure` imports from `domain` + external libs (SQLAlchemy, etc.)\n- `presentation` imports from `application` + `domain` + FastAPI\n\n---\n\n### Step 4 — Write your alembic migration with DATA CONTRACT\n\n```python\n\"\"\"add business_plans table\n\nDATA CONTRACT — business_plans table\n- idea_id: FK to ideas.id, unique — one plan per idea, always set\n- status: 'draft' | 'final', null means never generated\n- sections: JSONB dict with keys: idea, product, phases, financials, prototype, mvp\n  each section has: {content: str, status: 'idle'|'generating'|'draft'|'edited', last_edited_at: datetime|null}\n- generated_at: datetime, null until first generation completes\n\"\"\"\nimport sqlalchemy as sa\nfrom alembic import op\n\nrevision = \"0007\"\ndown_revision = \"0006\"\n\ndef upgrade() -> None:\n    op.create_table(\n        \"business_plans\",\n        sa.Column(\"id\", sa.String(36), primary_key=True),\n        sa.Column(\"idea_id\", sa.String(36), sa.ForeignKey(\"ideas.id\", ondelete=\"CASCADE\"),\n                  nullable=False, unique=True),\n        sa.Column(\"status\", sa.String(20), nullable=True),\n        sa.Column(\"sections\", sa.JSON(), nullable=True),\n        sa.Column(\"generated_at\", sa.DateTime(), nullable=True),\n        sa.Column(\"created_at\", sa.DateTime(), nullable=False),\n        sa.Column(\"updated_at\", sa.DateTime(), nullable=False),\n    )\n\ndef downgrade() -> None:\n    op.drop_table(\"business_plans\")\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 5 — Register your router in main.py\n\n```python\n# backend/app/main.py\nfrom app.modules.business_plan.presentation.router import router as bp_router\n\napp.include_router(bp_router)\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 6 — Run locally\n\n```bash\n# 1. Start the database\ndocker compose up -d\n\n# 2. Apply your new migration\nconda activate aieng\ncd backend\nalembic upgrade head\n\n# 3. Start backend\nuvicorn app.main:app --reload\n\n# 4. Start frontend (separate terminal)\ncd frontend\nnpm run dev\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Cross-Module Data Access Pattern (Port + Adapter)\n\nWhen your module needs data from another module's table, define what you need as an interface in your own `domain/ports.py` and implement it in `infrastructure/` with your own ORM model. **Never import another module's repository or use case.**\n\n**`business_plan/domain/ports.py`** — define the interface:\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Protocol\n\n@dataclass\nclass IdeaSnapshot:\n    id: str\n    idea_description: str | None\n    domain: str | None\n    review_summary: str | None\n    onboarding_completed: bool\n\nclass IIdeaReader(Protocol):\n    async def get_idea_snapshot(self, idea_id: str) -> IdeaSnapshot | None:\n        ...\n```\n\n**`business_plan/infrastructure/idea_reader.py`** — implement with your own ORM model:\n\n```python\nfrom sqlalchemy import select\nfrom sqlalchemy.ext.asyncio import AsyncSession\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import DeclarativeBase, mapped_column, Mapped\nimport sqlalchemy as sa\n\nfrom app.modules.business_plan.domain.ports import IdeaSnapshot\n\n# Your own ORM model — points to the shared ideas table\n# Do NOT import IdeaORM from app.modules.ideas\nclass Base(DeclarativeBase): pass\n\nclass IdeaReadModel(Base):\n    __tablename__ = \"ideas\"\n    id: Mapped[str] = mapped_column(sa.String(36), primary_key=True)\n    idea_description: Mapped[str | None] = mapped_column(sa.Text)\n    domain: Mapped[str | None] = mapped_column(sa.String(255))\n    review_summary: Mapped[str | None] = mapped_column(sa.Text)\n    onboarding_completed: Mapped[bool] = mapped_column(sa.Boolean)\n\n\nclass PostgresIdeaReader:\n    def __init__(self, session: AsyncSession):\n        self._session = session\n\n    async def get_idea_snapshot(self, idea_id: str) -> IdeaSnapshot | None:\n        result = await self._session.get(IdeaReadModel, idea_id)\n        if not result:\n            return None\n        return IdeaSnapshot(\n            id=result.id,\n            idea_description=result.idea_description,\n            domain=result.domain,\n            review_summary=result.review_summary,\n            onboarding_completed=result.onboarding_completed,\n        )\n```\n\n**`business_plan/application/use_cases.py`** — depends only on the port:\n\n```python\nfrom app.modules.business_plan.domain.ports import IIdeaReader, IBusinessPlanRepo\n\nclass GeneratePlanUseCase:\n    def __init__(self, repo: IBusinessPlanRepo, idea_reader: IIdeaReader):\n        self._repo = repo\n        self._idea_reader = idea_reader\n\n    async def execute(self, idea_id: str) -> BusinessPlan:\n        idea = await self._idea_reader.get_idea_snapshot(idea_id)\n        if not idea or not idea.onboarding_completed:\n            raise ValueError(\"Onboarding not complete\")\n        # ... generate plan using idea.review_summary, idea.domain, etc.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Auth Injection Pattern\n\nAuth is always injected at the router (presentation) layer. It never goes deeper.\n\n```python\n# ✅ Correct — auth only at presentation layer\n# business_plan/presentation/router.py\n\nfrom app.modules.auth.presentation.dependencies import get_current_user\n\n@router.post(\"/generate\", response_model=BusinessPlanResponse)\nasync def generate_plan(\n    current_user: User = Depends(get_current_user),   # ← inject here\n    session: AsyncSession = Depends(get_session),\n) -> BusinessPlanResponse:\n    idea_id = current_user.current_idea_id\n    if not idea_id:\n        raise_404(\"No active idea\")\n    idea_reader = PostgresIdeaReader(session)\n    repo = PostgresBusinessPlanRepo(session)\n    plan = await GeneratePlanUseCase(repo, idea_reader).execute(idea_id)\n    return BusinessPlanResponse(**plan.__dict__)\n```\n\n```python\n# ❌ Wrong — auth imported inside use case or domain\nclass GeneratePlanUseCase:\n    async def execute(self, ...):\n        from app.modules.auth.infrastructure.repository import PostgresUserRepository  # never\n        from app.modules.auth.domain.entities import User  # never (except as a plain dataclass in shared)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Stage Progression Pattern\n\n`ideas` module owns all stage status. When your module finishes its work, signal completion through the ideas API — never write to `idea_stages` directly.\n\n```python\n# ✅ Correct — call the ideas API endpoint from your module's use case\n# (or frontend calls it directly after your module's work completes)\n\n# HTTP call from backend use case, or from frontend after success:\nPUT /api/ideas/{idea_id}/stages/stage3\nContent-Type: application/json\nAuthorization: Bearer <token>\n\n{\n  \"status\": \"completed\",\n  \"stage_data\": { \"plan_id\": \"abc-123\" }\n}\n```\n\n```python\n# ❌ Wrong — directly mutating idea_stages from your module\nfrom sqlalchemy import update\n# in business_plan/infrastructure/repository.py:\nawait session.execute(\n    update(IdeaStageORM).where(IdeaStageORM.stage_key == \"stage3\")  # never\n)\n```\n\n**Why:** The `ideas` module enforces stage transition rules (e.g., can't complete stage3 if stage2 is still locked). Going around it breaks those invariants.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Local Dev Setup\n\n```bash\n# Terminal 1 — Start database\ndocker compose up -d\n\n# Terminal 2 — Backend\nconda activate aieng\ncd backend\nalembic upgrade head          # run all migrations including your new one\nuvicorn app.main:app --reload  # hot-reload on file change\n\n# Terminal 3 — Frontend\ncd frontend\nnpm run dev\n```\n\n**Switching branches (different DB schemas):**\n\n```bash\ngit checkout feature/your-branch\n\n# Wipe DB and re-migrate (only needed if schemas are incompatible)\nconda activate aieng\ncd backend\npython -c \"\nimport asyncio\nfrom sqlalchemy.ext.asyncio import create_async_engine\nfrom shared.config import settings\n\nasync def wipe():\n    engine = create_async_engine(settings.database_url)\n    async with engine.begin() as conn:\n        await conn.run_sync(lambda c: c.execute(\n            __import__('sqlalchemy').text('DROP SCHEMA public CASCADE; CREATE SCHEMA public;')\n        ))\n    await engine.dispose()\n\nasyncio.run(wipe())\n\"\nalembic upgrade head\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. Team Agreements\n\n| Rule | Why |\n|------|-----|\n| One module writes to each table | Prevents conflicting writes and lost updates |\n| Other modules read via their own ORM `ReadModel` | No import coupling — modules stay independently deployable |\n| `DATA CONTRACT` docstring in every migration | New developers can onboard without reading Python code |\n| Auth injected at router only, never in domain/application | Auth is infrastructure, not business logic |\n| Stage status updated via `PUT /api/ideas/{id}/stages/{key}`, never direct DB write | `ideas` module controls transition invariants |\n| `shared/` = `config.py`, `database.py`, `exceptions.py` only | Prevents business logic from leaking into shared utilities |\n| Module folder always has: `domain/`, `application/`, `infrastructure/`, `presentation/` | Consistent structure — every dev knows where to find things |\n| Brainstorm schema + API contract with Claude Code before writing code | Agree on the DATA CONTRACT before a line of implementation is written |\n\n---\n\n## 9. Quick Reference — What Each Module Owns\n\n| Module | Writes to | Reads from | Stage |\n|--------|-----------|------------|-------|\n| `auth` | `users` | `users` | cross-cutting |\n| `ideas` | `ideas`, `idea_stages` | `ideas`, `idea_stages`, `users` | stage ledger |\n| `onboarding` | `ideas` (subset of columns) | `ideas` | Stage 1 |\n| `research` | `research_jobs`, `research_reports` | `ideas` (own ReadModel) | Stage 2 |\n| `business_plan` *(planned)* | `business_plans` | `ideas` (own ReadModel) | Stage 3 |\n| `mentors` *(planned)* | `mentor_matches` | `ideas`, `business_plans` (own ReadModels) | Stage 6 |"}
	],
	"edges":[]
}